/* ============================================================================
polaris-drift.epher -- Polaris and the pole across five centuries

Precesses Polaris's J2000 catalogue position to 1900, 1950, 2000,
2050 and 2100 with the rigorous method of Meeus ch. 21 (the same
recipe precession-j2000.epher carries, as local defs with
star-tonight.epher's Polaris coordinates as the anchor), prints the
declination and the polar distance 90 - dec for each era, then scans
the model for the epoch of closest approach - the moment Meeus's
"Mathematical Astronomy Morsels" (the chapter on the declination of
Polaris) discusses, when the pole swings nearest this star before
leaving it behind for good.

Accuracy: the IAU-1976 precession only. Polaris's proper motion
(about 44 mas/yr in RA, -12 mas/yr in dec) is omitted; over the two
centuries bridged here it moves the computed positions by a few
arcminutes at the edge, which is the honest error at 1900 and 2150.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/astronomy/stars/polaris-drift.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\astronomy\stars\polaris-drift.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/astronomy/stars/polaris-drift.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above

Knobs: the J2000 anchor position below; the era list is in the
results but the scan range is in the model section.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs ----------------------------------------------------------------
const pol_ra = hms2deg(2, 31, 49.09)    // Polaris, J2000 (SIMBAD)
const pol_dec = dms2deg(89, 15, 50.8)
const scan_from = 2050                  // closest-approach scan range
const scan_to = 2150

// ---- Meeus ch. 21: declination of date from J2000 ------------------------------
def cent0(j) = (j - jd(2000, 1, 1, 12)) / 36525
def sig(tt, T) = ((2306.2181 + 1.39656 * T - 0.000139 * T ^ 2) * tt + (0.30188 - 0.000344 * T) * tt ^ 2 + 0.017988 * tt ^ 3) / 3600
def phi(tt, T) = ((2004.3109 - 0.8533 * T - 0.000217 * T ^ 2) * tt - (0.42665 + 0.000217 * T) * tt ^ 2 - 0.041833 * tt ^ 3) / 3600
def dec_of(ra, dec, j) = deg(asin(sin(rad(phi((j - jd(2000, 1, 1, 12)) / 36525, cent0(j)))) * cos(rad(dec)) * cos(rad(ra + sig((j - jd(2000, 1, 1, 12)) / 36525, cent0(j)))) + cos(rad(phi((j - jd(2000, 1, 1, 12)) / 36525, cent0(j)))) * sin(rad(dec))))
def dec_at(y) = dec_of(pol_ra, pol_dec, jd(2000, 1, 1, 12) + 365.25 * (y - 2000))   // Julian epoch y.0
// (jd needs whole days, so epochs run from J2000.0 in Julian years of 365.25 d;
// the scan's fractional years need the same footing)
def pdist(y) = 90 - dec_at(y)                          // polar distance, deg

// ---- closest approach: coarse scan then fine, state packed in a list -----------
v = {scan_from, scan_from, pdist(scan_from)}
while v[1] <= scan_to do v = {v[1] + 1, if pdist(v[1]) < v[3] then v[1] else v[2], min(v[3], pdist(v[1]))}
w = {v[2] - 2, v[2], pdist(v[2])}
while w[1] <= v[2] + 2 do w = {w[1] + 0.05, if pdist(w[1]) < w[3] then w[1] else w[2], min(w[3], pdist(w[1]))}

// ---- results ------------------------------------------------------------------
print("1900: dec", deg2dms(dec_at(1900)), "polar dist", 60 * pdist(1900), "arcmin")
print("1950: dec", deg2dms(dec_at(1950)), "polar dist", 60 * pdist(1950), "arcmin")
print("2000: dec", deg2dms(dec_at(2000)), "polar dist", 60 * pdist(2000), "arcmin")
print("2050: dec", deg2dms(dec_at(2050)), "polar dist", 60 * pdist(2050), "arcmin")
print("2100: dec", deg2dms(dec_at(2100)), "polar dist", 60 * pdist(2100), "arcmin")
print("closest approach:", w[2], "at polar distance", 60 * w[3], "arcmin")

/* Cross-check: Morsels puts the closest approach in the early 2100s
at about 27 arcminutes, and the model's self-consistency shows in the
round trip - precessing the 2100 position back to J2000 must return
the catalogue value (precession-j2000.epher demonstrates the round
trip on theta Hydrae). The table also shows the observing benefit:
the pointing correction for Polaris shrinks from 1950 on and is
smallest right around 2100. */

/* ---- expected output
= 37.9545416667
= 89.2641111111
= 2050
= 2150
= {2050, 2050, 0.545378816109}
= {2100, 2102, 0.4592454087}
= 1900: dec 88° 46' 25" polar dist 73.5822402283 arcmin
= 1950: dec 89° 1' 43" polar dist 58.2844546384 arcmin
= 2000: dec 89° 15' 51" polar dist 6623/150 arcmin
= 2050: dec 89° 27' 17" polar dist 32.7227289666 arcmin
= 2100: dec 89° 32' 26" polar dist 27.5662953448 arcmin
= closest approach: 2102.4 at polar distance 27.554384622 arcmin
*/